SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] running — Easy aerobic jog of 45–55 min at 6:30
 –7:00/km (HR ≤138 bpm)\;
DESCRIPTION:Easy aerobic jog of 45–55 min at 6:30–7:00/km (HR ≤138 bp
 m)\; stop immediately if cardiac drift occurs. No distance target.\n\nReas
 on: After weeks of near-zero training due to illness (self-reported 15 Aug
 ) and consistently depleted body battery\, jumping straight to a 180-min l
 ong run poses a significant injury and overload risk — a short easy run 
 rebuilds the habit safely.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260827-1@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260822T122246Z
DTSTART:20260827T070000
DTEND:20260827T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] cycling — Cap at 50 min at the lower end of Z2 (128
 –145W)\, flat effort
DESCRIPTION:Cap at 50 min at the lower end of Z2 (128–145W)\, flat effort
 \, HR ≤138 bpm. Stop at any sign of fatigue accumulation.\n\nReason: Two
  weeks post-illness with only one low-load session completed\, jumping to 
 a full 90-min Z2 ride risks extending the recovery hole — 50 min achieve
 s the aerobic stimulus without the fatigue cost.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260829-2@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260822T122246Z
DTSTART:20260829T070000
DTEND:20260829T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] cycling — Keep the 60 min easy ride (≤130W) as th
 e sole session for th
DESCRIPTION:Keep the 60 min easy ride (≤130W) as the sole session for the
  day\; drop the added evening swim entirely this week.\n\nReason: Doubling
  up on the same day (ride + 2\,000–2\,500m swim) is excessive load for a
 n athlete only just returning from illness — the Friday race-simulation 
 swim is the higher-priority swim session and must be protected by keeping 
 Saturday light.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260830-3@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260822T122246Z
DTSTART:20260830T070000
DTEND:20260830T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] running — Easy aerobic run of 60–75 min at 6:30
 –7:00/km (HR ≤140 bpm)\;
DESCRIPTION:Easy aerobic run of 60–75 min at 6:30–7:00/km (HR ≤140 bp
 m)\; extend to 90 min only if the first hour felt genuinely comfortable wi
 th no drift.\n\nReason: A 19km long run in Week 10\, following three weeks
  of essentially no running due to illness\, is an acute load spike that sh
 arply increases injury risk — a stepped re-introduction to long running 
 is essential before returning to full distances.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260903-4@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260822T122246Z
DTSTART:20260903T070000
DTEND:20260903T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] running — Replace with 35 min easy run at 6:15–6:
 30/km (HR ≤142 bpm) p
DESCRIPTION:Replace with 35 min easy run at 6:15–6:30/km (HR ≤142 bpm) 
 plus 2×5 min at 5:50/km with 3 min easy recovery — no full tempo block 
 yet.\n\nReason: Introducing sustained tempo effort in Week 11 is premature
  given the detraining and illness block\; short controlled pickups maintai
 n running economy without the physiological demand of a full tempo run on 
 undertrained legs.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260905-5@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260822T122246Z
DTSTART:20260905T070000
DTEND:20260905T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] cycling — Cap at 150 min (approx 65–70km) at 128
 –155W\; no Z3 blocks. E
DESCRIPTION:Cap at 150 min (approx 65–70km) at 128–155W\; no Z3 blocks.
  Extend to 180 min only if HR remains stable and RPE is genuinely easy thr
 oughout.\n\nReason: A 240-min long ride would be the biggest single traini
 ng load since illness and risks a setback three weeks before the Half Ocea
 nman — stepping up to 150 min first maintains the build without over-str
 essing an athlete still re-establishing base fitness.\n\nAI coaching 2026-
 08-22
END:VEVENT
